a dense forest of pine and silver birch  trees lines the road to the remote town  of verache in northwest ukraine close to  the border with belarus  visible in the distance is the rivne  power plant ukraine's oldest nuclear  facility some people worry that russian  forces could try to seize this plant  because moscow's troops have already  taken control of zapariza one of  ukraine's four operational nuclear  facilities as well as chernobyl  it's nuclear terrorism chernobyl is  decommissioned but it stores nuclear  waste that needs to be cool  but there are power cuts  for zappo  it is europe's biggest nuclear plant it  has six reactors so when russian forces  take it in this sort of attack there are  many risks  reactors can be damaged and things can  go wrong  things nearly went wrong here on monday  after ukrainian troops tried to swat a  nearby attack by russian forces  there was a missile that flew above here  to shoot down an enemy drone  so this is happening here  there are risks  russian shelling also damaged a nuclear  facility in hakeev last week  moscow's targeting of nuclear sites is a  sinister and dangerous twist in the war  that could further threaten europe and  is fueling anxiety here  more than 7 000 people work at the power  plant so most people in this town are  connected to it in some way and nearly  everyone is acutely aware of what a  nuclear disaster could mean  the mayor of arash is an engineer who  worked at the rifne plant for 25 years  he says everything's been done to  protect it  we know how dangerous nuclear energy is  for the world we've seen fukushima and  chernobyl in viresh there's all sorts of  work at the plant and many skilled  people like me an engineer understand  the gravity of a nuclear site not being  secure and controlled it can be a  catastrophe  a memorial in the town centre  commemorates the victims of chernobyl  the world's worst nuclear accident took  place nearly 40 years ago people here  want the international community to do  more to help safeguard ukraine's nuclear  sites so that a grim part of history  will not be repeated  natasha butler al-jazeera  north west ukraine
 

